---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/medical-imaging">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Medical imaging is <strong>applied physics turned diagnostic</strong>: X-ray absorption,
    magnetic resonance, and sound echoes, each revealing different tissue properties. Choosing a
    modality is choosing a trade-off — resolution vs radiation vs speed vs cost — and the
    reading of images is itself a cognitive skill under AI transformation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four modalities and their physics-to-diagnosis logic" widget="equationStep" config={imagingCfg} />

  <h2>The AI transformation, already underway</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Detection models are FDA-cleared:</strong> algorithms flag lung nodules on CT, haemorrhages on head CT, diabetic retinopathy on retinal photos — several outperform average readers in narrow tasks.</li>
    <li><strong>The radiologist's changing job:</strong> less pixel-hunting, more integration — deciding which study, correlating with clinical context, owning the final call. "AI won't replace radiologists; radiologists using AI will replace those who don't" is the working consensus.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitfalls are documented:</strong> models trained on one hospital's scanners underperform elsewhere; subtle distribution shifts (patient position, machine vendor) degrade accuracy silently — the evaluation lesson's warnings, medically priced.</li>
    <li><strong>Imaging's history is a physics victory lap:</strong> Röntgen's first X-ray (his wife's hand, 1895), CT's Nobel (1979), MRI's Nobel (2003) — each modality began as a physics experiment that medicine adopted within years.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Röntgen (1895); Brant & Helms, Fundamentals of Diagnostic Radiology; FDA-cleared AI device list; Topol, Deep Medicine (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
